'use strict';
const {course,section:s,worked:w}=require('./web-helper');
const units=[
{id:'html-heading-outline',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Build document structure with headings and landmarks',introduction:'A hospital publishes appointment instructions with preparation, arrival and follow-up sections. A visually large phrase is not automatically a heading: semantic structure helps people scan, navigate with assistive technology and understand how the information fits together.',sections:[s('Give the document a meaningful hierarchy','A document normally starts with <!doctype html>, an html element with an appropriate lang, a head for metadata and a body for content. Use a concise title for the page, then organize the visible main topic with a clear heading hierarchy. An h1 identifies a top-level topic; h2 and h3 can identify nested sections. Choose heading rank for structure, not the desired font size.','Landmarks describe major regions: main for the page’s primary content, nav for major navigation and appropriately placed header/footer elements for introductory or closing information. Use one visible primary main region. Repeated site navigation and page-specific content should not be indistinguishable strings of divs.'),s('Check meaning as well as syntax','A page can parse even when its hierarchy is confusing. Review headings as a list: does each name identify its section, and does nesting follow the content relationships? A long page may need a skip link to main content and distinguishable labels for multiple navigation regions.','The example uses Python’s HTMLParser to collect heading tags from controlled markup. It verifies only the authored tag sequence. It does not compute a browser accessibility tree or establish that every screen reader exposes the same navigation behavior; those require real interface checks.')],worked:w('from html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass Headings(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__(); self.tags=[]\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        if tag in {"h1","h2","h3"}: self.tags.append(tag)\np=Headings(); p.feed("""<main><h1>Appointments</h1><h2>Before arrival</h2><h3>Documents</h3></main>""")\nprint(" > ".join(p.tags))','h1 > h2 > h3','Inspect the explicit heading hierarchy in a small appointment page.',['Parse the controlled HTML text.','Collect only heading start tags.','Compare the resulting order with the intended content hierarchy.'],'The parser observes tags, not their complete contextual accessibility semantics. A correct sequence alone does not guarantee clear heading text.'),mistakes:['Choosing heading rank only to obtain a font size.','Assuming valid parsing proves a useful document outline.'],takeaways:['Use heading ranks to express content relationships.','Keep primary content and navigation regions meaningful.'],quiz:[
['What should determine a heading’s rank?','Its structural role','Only the desired text size','Only its colour','Only its character count','Heading ranks express hierarchy, while CSS can control visual appearance.','Typography can be styled independently.','Colour does not define content nesting.','Length does not determine structural rank.'],
['Which element identifies primary page content?','main','A div named large','A br before the title','A font-size declaration','The main element expresses the primary content region under HTML semantics.','A class name alone does not create that landmark.','A line break is not a content landmark.','CSS typography does not identify a main region.'],
['Where does the document title belong?','In head metadata','Inside every image alt','Only in a CSS comment','Only in an empty script','The title element belongs in the head and identifies the document.','Alternative text describes image purpose.','Comments do not provide title metadata.','An empty script has no title semantics.'],
['An appointment page uses h4 for its main topic because it looks small. What should change?','Choose rank by hierarchy','Add more line breaks','Move all content into a table','Remove every heading','Heading rank should reflect document structure; size belongs to presentation.','Line breaks do not correct hierarchy.','Tables are for tabular data, not this structure.','Removing structure makes navigation worse.'],
['A page has two navigation regions. What helps users distinguish them?','Meaningful region labels','Identical unexplained icons','Only different border widths','A hidden random identifier','Distinct accessible names can clarify the purpose of repeated navigation landmarks.','Icons without meaning may confuse.','Borders are not robust names.','Random identifiers do not explain purpose.'],
['A long portal repeats navigation before main content. What can improve keyboard access?','A working skip link','A title made of spaces','A disabled Tab key','An image-only heading','A skip link can let users bypass repeated content to a suitable target.','An empty title harms identification.','Disabling navigation blocks access.','Image-only headings need their own careful alternatives.'],
['An HTML parser accepts a confusing heading sequence. What follows?','Review still is needed','Its information structure is certified','Every screen reader was tested','The page must be secure','Syntax acceptance is not proof of usable semantics or interface behavior.','Certification exceeds parser evidence.','No assistive technology ran.','Parsing does not validate security.'],
['The fixture prints h1 > h2 > h3. What did it directly check?','The authored tag order','All spoken navigation output','The page’s visual contrast','The server’s permissions','The example collects specific tags from supplied HTML and reports their sequence.','It does not run a screen reader.','It does not compute styles.','It does not contact an authorization service.']
]},
{id:'html-semantic-controls',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Choose semantic elements for actions and content',introduction:'A customer portal needs navigation to invoices and an action to submit a refund request. Similar visual styling does not make a link and a button interchangeable. Native elements communicate purpose and supply behavior that generic containers do not.',sections:[s('Match the element to the task','Use an anchor with href for navigation to a resource. Use a button for an action such as submitting a form or opening a dialog; choose its type deliberately because a button inside a form can default to submission. Use lists for related items and tables for genuinely tabular relationships, with appropriate headers.','A div or span is a generic container useful for grouping or styling. Adding an onclick handler does not automatically provide keyboard operation, focus behavior or the appropriate accessible role. Prefer native semantics before adding ARIA; a role alone does not implement all expected interactions.'),s('Preserve meaning when design changes','The same button may have many visual styles without losing action semantics. Link text should describe the destination in context; repeated “click here” labels make lists of links hard to interpret. Avoid nesting interactive controls such as a button inside a link, because the competing behaviors become confusing or invalid.','The example classifies a small declared set of tasks. It is a design check, not an HTML conformance validator or a browser event test. A finished control must still be tested with keyboard, focus indication and the actual operation’s success/error states.')],worked:w('tasks = [("view invoice", "navigate"),("send request", "action"),("related items", "collection")]\nchoose = {"navigate":"a with href", "action":"button", "collection":"ul or ol"}\nfor label, kind in tasks:\n    print(label + ": " + choose[kind])','view invoice: a with href\nsend request: button\nrelated items: ul or ol','Choose suitable native HTML structures for three customer-portal tasks.',['Identify whether each task navigates, acts or groups items.','Apply the declared semantic mapping.','Review whether the actual interface needs additional behavior tests.'],'The mapping is a bounded design aid; it does not verify rendered keyboard behavior or every possible interaction.'),mistakes:['Treating an onclick div as automatically equivalent to a button.','Using layout tables where a meaningful list or CSS layout is appropriate.'],takeaways:['Choose native semantics before recreating interaction.','Keep action, navigation and content relationships clear.'],quiz:[
['Which element is appropriate for navigating to an invoice URL?','An anchor with href','An unnamed span','A button with no behavior','A blank div','Anchors with destinations express resource navigation.','A span has no navigation semantics by itself.','A button is an action control.','A blank container does not identify a destination.'],
['Which element normally performs an interface action?','button','A decorative br','An empty title','A CSS custom property','A native button provides action-control semantics and standard interaction behavior.','A line break is content formatting.','Title is document metadata.','A token does not create a control.'],
['What do div and span provide by default?','Generic grouping','Automatic form validation','Automatic keyboard buttons','A database table schema','Generic containers have no specialized action or landmark meaning by default.','Validation needs suitable controls and rules.','A container is not automatically a button.','HTML containers are not database schemas.'],
['A button inside a form should not submit it. What should be specified?','type="button"','Only a shorter text label','Only a blue background','Only an empty href','An explicit button type avoids unintended default submission behavior.','Labels do not set submission type.','Styling does not determine form behavior.','Buttons do not use href for type selection.'],
['A developer adds role="button" to a div. What still needs implementation?','Interaction behavior','Only a DNS record','Only a new domain','Only a title element','ARIA semantics do not automatically implement keyboard, focus and activation behavior.','DNS is unrelated.','A domain does not implement interaction.','Document titles do not supply button behavior.'],
['A product list has a button nested inside a link. What is the concern?','Competing interactive semantics','The price must become zero','DNS cannot resolve the page','CSS can no longer be loaded','Nesting interactive controls creates invalid or confusing interaction structures.','The markup does not compute prices.','DNS is independent of this nesting.','Stylesheets can still load even when semantics are flawed.'],
['Several links all say “click here.” What can improve their usability?','Descriptive destination text','The same label in uppercase','Only a larger underline','A random numeric title','Useful link text explains destinations, particularly when encountered out of surrounding prose.','Capitalization does not clarify destination.','An underline signals a link but not its purpose.','Random text does not explain the resource.'],
['A parser maps a refund task to button. What remains to test?','Actual keyboard activation','The literal mapping key','The dictionary entry exists','The supplied label string','A design mapping does not execute browser interaction or verify focus and activation behavior.','The key is already part of the fixture.','The entry is directly supplied.','The label is fixture data.']
]},
{id:'html-content-regions',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Organize repeated navigation and self-contained content',introduction:'A university news portal has site navigation, a main news story and related links. Landmarks and content elements should describe those relationships rather than merely reproduce a grid of visual boxes.',sections:[s('Choose regions by meaning','Use nav for major navigation collections rather than wrapping every incidental link. A page-level header can introduce the site or page; a footer can contain relevant closing information. Main identifies the dominant page content, while aside can hold tangential supporting content. The precise accessibility-landmark mapping can depend on nesting and browser/assistive-technology behavior.','An article is suitable for a self-contained composition that could reasonably stand on its own, such as a news story. A section groups a thematic part of content, usually with a heading. Neither means “any box that needs padding”; generic wrappers remain appropriate when no stronger semantic meaning is intended.'),s('Use stable navigation targets','A skip link can refer to #main-content when an element has id="main-content". IDs must be unique within the document. Duplicate targets make navigation and label relationships ambiguous; merely adding more headings does not repair incorrect references.','The parser fixture counts IDs and checks a declared skip target. It flags a concrete authoring defect but does not prove a complete accessibility audit. Real testing must confirm focus placement, visible focus, responsive order and sensible landmark names.')],worked:w('from html.parser import HTMLParser\nfrom collections import Counter\nclass IDs(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self): super().__init__(); self.ids=[]\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        self.ids += [v for k,v in attrs if k=="id"]\np=IDs(); p.feed("""<a href=\"#main-content\">Skip</a><main id=\"main-content\"></main><aside id=\"notes\"></aside>""")\nprint(Counter(p.ids)["main-content"] == 1)\nprint(len(p.ids) == len(set(p.ids)))','True\nTrue','Check unique target identifiers in a synthetic page skeleton.',['Collect id attributes.','Confirm exactly one main-content target.','Check whether any identifier is duplicated.'],'The checks establish identifier uniqueness in supplied markup, not focus transfer behavior in a real browser.'),mistakes:['Using section or article only because they create a visual box.','Repeating the same id on several regions.'],takeaways:['Make regions describe content relationships.','Keep internal navigation targets unique and test their behavior.'],quiz:[
['What is article intended to represent?','A self-contained composition','Any padded box','Only an invisible wrapper','Only a navigation menu','An article can represent content that reasonably stands alone as a composition.','Padding is presentation.','Article has meaning beyond generic wrapping.','Major navigation has a different element.'],
['What is an id uniqueness requirement?','One value per document target','Every element must share it','Only CSS needs uniqueness','Duplicate ids are preferred','Repeated identical IDs create ambiguous document references.','Sharing defeats unique targeting.','Labels and links use IDs too.','Duplication creates defects rather than clarity.'],
['Which element can represent tangential supporting content?','aside','An empty meta tag','A script source path','A DNS address record','Aside expresses content related tangentially to the surrounding context.','Metadata is not a visible supporting region.','A script path is a resource reference.','DNS records do not structure documents.'],
['A skip link points to #main-content but no such id exists. What is wrong?','The internal target is missing','The domain is necessarily invalid','The title must be too long','The HTTP method is always wrong','Fragment navigation requires the intended document target to exist.','Host validity is separate.','Title length does not create the target.','The issue is not established as a method problem.'],
['A layout wraps every decorative card in nav. What should be reviewed?','Navigation purpose','Only the card shadow','Only the image format','Only the Git author','Nav has a navigation meaning and should not be assigned merely for layout.','Visual effects do not establish semantics.','Formats do not define region purpose.','Authorship does not choose the right element.'],
['Two page regions use the same main-content id. What can fail?','Unique target references','Only image compression','Only DNS caching','Only server log rotation','Duplicate IDs can make links, labels and scripts address an unintended element.','Compression is unrelated.','DNS operates outside document IDs.','Log rotation does not use these region identities.'],
['A news story stands alone and has its own heading. Which element may fit?','article','Only br elements','Only a blank span','Only a CSS comment','A self-contained story is an appropriate use of article semantics.','Line breaks do not express composition.','Generic spans do not add the same meaning.','Comments do not structure rendered content.'],
['The ID parser prints two True values. What is still needed for a skip link?','Browser keyboard verification','Another equality comparison only','A longer id spelling','A database migration','Identifier checks do not establish focus behavior, visibility or useful keyboard navigation.','Repeating the same check adds no browser evidence.','Length alone does not improve behavior.','No database change is needed for the interaction test.']
]},
{id:'html-labels-constraints',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Build labeled forms with useful native constraints',introduction:'A clinic intake form asks for a name, contact email and appointment date. Clear labels and suitable input types improve completion, but browser validation is a usability layer rather than proof that the server received trustworthy data.',sections:[s('Connect visible names to controls','A label with for="email" associates with a control whose unique id is email. The name attribute serves a different purpose: it identifies the submitted field. A placeholder may give an example, but it disappears during entry and is not a substitute for a persistent label. Group related choices with fieldset and legend where appropriate.','Choose types such as email, date or number for the data and interaction required. Native constraints include required, minlength, maxlength, min, max and pattern where applicable. A telephone number is often text-like rather than an arithmetic number; leading zeros and international formatting matter.'),s('Validate kindly and enforce authoritatively','Explain required information and expected formats before submission. Do not reject legitimate names merely because they contain spaces or non-ASCII characters. Keep privacy in mind: request only fields necessary for the actual workflow.','Native browser constraints can prevent ordinary invalid submissions, but callers can bypass them. The server must validate types, allowed fields, lengths, meaning and authorization for the real operation. The Python fixture checks label/id associations in controlled markup; it does not run the browser’s constraint-validation algorithm.')],worked:w('from html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass Form(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self): super().__init__(); self.labels=[]; self.ids=set()\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        a=dict(attrs)\n        if tag=="label": self.labels.append(a.get("for"))\n        if tag=="input" and a.get("id"): self.ids.add(a["id"])\np=Form(); p.feed("""<label for=\"email\">Email</label><input id=\"email\" name=\"contact_email\" type=\"email\" required>""")\nprint(all(x in p.ids for x in p.labels))\nprint(len(p.labels))','True\n1','Verify a declared label refers to an input ID.',['Parse label targets and control IDs.','Compare each association.','Count the explicitly labeled fields.'],'The example checks a narrow association only; it does not audit every accessible name or execute native validation.'),mistakes:['Using placeholder text as the only label.','Confusing an input id with its submitted field name.'],takeaways:['Give controls persistent understandable labels.','Use appropriate constraints while retaining server validation.'],quiz:[
['Which attribute connects a label to a control id?','for','Only name','Only placeholder','Only maxlength','An explicit label’s for value refers to the target control’s id.','Name identifies submitted fields.','Placeholder is a transient hint.','Maxlength constrains text length.'],
['What is the input name mainly used for?','The submitted field key','The page’s title metadata','The DNS hostname','The CSS cascade origin','Named successful controls contribute key/value entries under their name during form submission.','Titles belong to document metadata.','DNS is separate naming infrastructure.','Name does not choose a cascade origin.'],
['Why is a placeholder insufficient as the only label?','It is not a persistent name','It guarantees server validation','It supplies all error messages','It creates a fieldset legend','A placeholder is a hint that can disappear and does not replace an understandable associated label.','It cannot enforce server rules.','Errors need separate handling.','It does not create grouped-choice semantics.'],
['A phone field drops its leading zero when treated as a number. What should be reviewed?','The field’s data meaning','Only the page background','Only the form’s position','Only the logo size','Phone identifiers often need textual formatting rather than numeric arithmetic.','Background does not preserve identifiers.','Position does not determine data meaning.','Logo dimensions are unrelated.'],
['A form marks email required. What must the server still do?','Validate the received request','Trust every direct API call','Ignore unknown managed fields','Assume all browsers enforce it','Client constraints do not replace authoritative validation of actual submitted data.','Direct callers can bypass browser checks.','Unknown fields may need rejection or controlled handling.','Not all requests use the expected form.'],
['A name validator rejects every non-ASCII character. What is the concern?','Legitimate names may be excluded','The browser cannot show any text','All names require encryption in CSS','The label loses its for attribute','Overly restrictive assumptions can exclude valid users without a justified requirement.','Browsers can display Unicode text.','CSS is not the right data-protection mechanism.','Validation does not inherently remove association attributes.'],
['A signup asks for unrelated medical history. What should be questioned?','Collection necessity','Only the textarea colour','Only input border radius','Only the form’s filename','Data minimization starts with collecting only justified information for the workflow.','Styling does not justify collection.','Rounded borders do not change privacy need.','Filenames do not establish purpose.'],
['The association parser reports a matching for/id pair. What remains untested?','Actual browser validation','The supplied for string','The input id’s presence','The fixture’s label count','A parser association check does not run native validity behavior or the complete accessibility computation.','That string is parsed explicitly.','The id is collected explicitly.','The count is printed by the fixture.']
]},
{id:'html-form-submission',comp:'m2.c2',title:'Trace form fields into an HTTP submission',introduction:'An enrollment search form uses GET, while a registration form creates an application through POST. Understanding what gets submitted helps developers avoid missing fields, duplicate actions and private values embedded in URLs.',sections:[s('Method and target describe the operation','A form’s action identifies the submission destination. GET submission commonly encodes fields into the URL query and is suitable for safe retrieval such as search. POST sends form data in a request body under the chosen encoding and is commonly used for state-changing submissions. POST does not itself encrypt or authorize the data; HTTPS and server controls remain necessary.','Controls need names to contribute their values. Disabled controls are generally omitted; unchecked checkboxes are generally omitted, while a checked checkbox without an explicit value commonly uses on. Readonly and disabled have different submission behavior. Real successful-control rules include control type, selected values and the submitter, so inspect the actual request when debugging.'),s('Keep serialization separate from trust','For ordinary text forms the default encoding is application/x-www-form-urlencoded. File uploads require appropriate multipart/form-data handling and server-side validation. Do not place private credentials in GET queries or assume a hidden input is trusted because users do not normally see it.','The fixture encodes a supplied list of field pairs. It demonstrates repeated keys and URL encoding, not automatic extraction of all browser controls. Server business validation, CSRF protection where needed and duplicate-submission handling are separate from serialization.')],worked:w('from urllib.parse import urlencode, parse_qs\nfields=[("course","web basics"),("slot","morning"),("slot","evening")]\nencoded=urlencode(fields)\nprint(encoded)\nprint(parse_qs(encoded)["slot"])','course=web+basics&slot=morning&slot=evening\n[\'morning\', \'evening\']','Encode multiple selected class slots without collapsing repeated field names.',['Represent submitted fields as ordered pairs.','Encode values using a standard routine.','Parse the repeated slot field back into a list.'],'The fixture starts from selected field pairs. Browser successful-control selection and actual HTTP delivery are not executed.'),mistakes:['Assuming disabled fields will always be submitted.','Treating POST or hidden inputs as automatic security.'],takeaways:['Understand method, destination, field names and encoding together.','Serialization is not validation, authorization or encryption.'],quiz:[
['Which attribute chooses a form’s submission destination?','action','Only id','Only placeholder','Only autocomplete','The action attribute identifies where the form submission is directed.','Id identifies a document element.','Placeholder provides an input hint.','Autocomplete affects filling behavior.'],
['Which method commonly puts form data into the URL query?','GET','POST as a guarantee','Only DELETE','Only a CSS selector','GET form submission encodes its entries into the target URL query under normal behavior.','POST normally sends data in a body rather than making this guarantee.','DELETE is not the normal HTML form choice here.','CSS does not define HTTP method serialization.'],
['Does POST itself encrypt form data?','No, TLS is separate','Yes, every body is encrypted','Yes, no HTTPS is needed','Only if the input is hidden','POST describes request semantics/body handling, not encryption.','Bodies can be sent without TLS.','HTTPS remains needed for secure transport.','Visibility does not encrypt a value.'],
['A disabled course field is absent from a submission. What explains this?','Disabled controls are omitted','The server deleted the HTML','Every name attribute is ignored','The action becomes a fragment','Disabled controls generally do not contribute successful form entries.','No server deletion is established.','Names remain important for eligible controls.','Action routing is independent.'],
['A form lets users choose two slot checkboxes with the same name. What should the parser preserve?','Multiple submitted values','Only the first character','A single invented boolean always','The CSS selector specificity','Repeated keys can represent multiple selected values and need appropriate server parsing.','Truncation loses data.','The actual contract may require a list.','Styling is not form decoding.'],
['A private reset secret is submitted by GET. What is the privacy concern?','URL retention and exposure','POST would authorize it automatically','The secret becomes a font','The query cannot be logged','Query values can persist in history, logs and other URL-handling contexts.','POST does not supply authorization.','Rendering does not change credential authority.','Queries can be retained in logs.'],
['A form includes a file input. Which encoding commonly supports file upload?','multipart/form-data','Only text in a CSS rule','Only application/json automatically','Only an image alt value','Multipart encoding supports transmitting file data with other form entries under browser behavior.','CSS is not request-body encoding.','Native forms do not automatically choose JSON.','Alt values do not carry uploaded file bytes.'],
['The Python encoder accepts a hidden role field. What must the server decide?','Whether the actor may set it','Whether the key looks familiar only','Whether it is visually hidden only','Whether encoding succeeded only','Serialization does not grant authority to change managed fields such as role.','Names do not grant permission.','Hidden values remain caller-controlled.','Encoding success is not authorization.']
]},
{id:'html-form-errors',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Design understandable validation and grouped choices',introduction:'A financial-aid application can fail because a required field is missing or a server rule rejects an otherwise well-formed value. Good forms tell people what happened, preserve safe input and direct them to a useful next step.',sections:[s('Make instructions and errors actionable','Use visible instructions for required fields and important format expectations. Associate help or error text with the relevant control, for example with aria-describedby when appropriate. Error text should identify the problem and how to correct it without exposing private internals. Do not rely solely on colour or a transient tooltip.','Use fieldset and legend for related groups such as attendance choices. Radio buttons in one named group select one value, while independent checkboxes can represent several choices. Native behavior reduces the need to recreate keyboard patterns, but the complete page still needs testing.'),s('Handle browser and server outcomes consistently','A native validity check can catch a missing value before submission, while a server can reject an unavailable appointment after a race. Preserve nonsecret user entries where appropriate, move or announce focus in a deliberate accessible way, and avoid duplicating a state-changing action just because an error message was unclear.','The fixture evaluates declared requirements against synthetic values and produces field-specific messages. It is not the browser constraint-validation API and does not prove an accessible live announcement. Test keyboard order, focus after failure and actual message association in the rendered form.')],worked:w('values={"email":"", "course":"web"}\nrequired={"email":"Enter a contact email.", "course":"Choose a course."}\nerrors={key:message for key,message in required.items() if not values.get(key)}\nprint(errors)\nprint("email" in errors and "course" not in errors)','{\'email\': \'Enter a contact email.\'}\nTrue','Generate a specific missing-field message from controlled values.',['Read the declared required fields.','Check the synthetic values for absence.','Return only the relevant field messages.'],'This model checks missing values only. It does not validate email syntax, perform server permission checks or exercise live announcements.'),mistakes:['Showing only a red border after failure.','Erasing all useful input without a privacy or correctness reason.'],takeaways:['Make errors specific, associated and correctable.','Test native choices and failure recovery with keyboard interaction.'],quiz:[
['What helps explain a group of related form controls?','fieldset and legend','Only a decorative border','Only a random span','Only the document URL','Fieldset and legend can provide meaningful grouping and a group-level label.','Borders do not create semantic grouping.','Generic text may lack the association.','URLs do not label control groups.'],
['What should a validation error communicate?','A useful correction path','Only a hexadecimal colour','Only a stack trace','Only an unexplained number','Useful errors identify what needs correction in terms the user can act on.','Colour alone is insufficient.','Internal traces can confuse and leak details.','Unexplained codes may not help recovery.'],
['Which native controls commonly represent one choice from a named group?','Radio buttons','Independent headings','A line break sequence','A title element','Radio inputs sharing a name normally form a one-choice group.','Headings structure content.','Breaks do not create choice behavior.','Title is metadata.'],
['A required field fails and only its border changes. What should be added?','An associated error','Only another red shade','Only a wider margin','Only a faster animation','Users need a perceivable, understandable message linked to the problematic field.','More colour still may not explain the issue.','Spacing does not describe correction.','Motion does not identify the problem.'],
['An appointment became unavailable after browser validation passed. What should the UI handle?','The server’s current-state error','Assume the browser was authoritative','Retry the booking indefinitely','Delete the user’s account','Server state can change and needs a useful error/recovery flow.','Client checks do not freeze server state.','Unbounded retries can repeat effects or overload.','Account deletion is unrelated and destructive.'],
['A form deletes all nonsecret entries after one error. What is the usability concern?','Unnecessary re-entry','Automatic role escalation','Guaranteed certificate failure','A new DNS record conflict','Preserving appropriate safe input can reduce frustration while correcting specific failures.','Re-entry is not itself a role grant.','Form state does not establish TLS failure.','DNS is unrelated to field preservation.'],
['An error is visible but never reached or announced by keyboard users. What needs testing?','Focus/error association','Only screenshot sharpness','Only the page filename','Only the company logo','Accessible recovery requires actual navigation and appropriate association/announcement behavior.','Sharper images do not establish keyboard access.','Names do not test focus.','Branding does not expose the error.'],
['The missing-field model returns one email message. What did it not validate?','Real email syntax and delivery','Which fixture field was empty','The declared message text','The dictionary membership result','The code checks presence only; syntax, ownership and delivery require other checks.','The empty field is explicit input.','The text is supplied in the fixture.','The membership result is printed.']
]},
{id:'html-image-alternatives',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Write image alternatives that match purpose',introduction:'A logistics report uses a decorative divider, a delivery-status icon and a chart of late shipments. The right alternative text depends on what each image contributes to the surrounding content, not merely on the file name or appearance.',sections:[s('Describe function and equivalent information','An img normally needs an alt attribute. For an informative image, provide concise equivalent information relevant to its context. For a purely decorative image, alt="" lets assistive technologies ignore it. Omitting alt is different from deliberately providing an empty alternative and can produce unhelpful filename announcements.','If an image is the only content of a link or button, its alternative should communicate the control’s purpose or destination. Avoid repeating “image of” unless that distinction matters. Do not repeat an adjacent caption word for word when the same information is already appropriately available.'),s('Handle complex and time-based media thoughtfully','A complex chart may need a concise alt plus nearby explanation or an accessible data table carrying the important values and relationships. A long dump of every pixel is not an effective equivalent. Captions/transcripts and other alternatives may be needed for audio/video depending on the content and relevant accessibility requirements.','The fixture checks explicit alt presence and distinguishes empty decorative text from a useful function label. It is a narrow authoring check: only a human review of context can decide whether an alternative is genuinely equivalent and whether the image is truly decorative.')],worked:w('images=[{"purpose":"decorative", "alt":""},{"purpose":"link", "alt":"View shipment status"}]\nprint(all("alt" in image for image in images))\nprint(images[0]["alt"] == "")\nprint(images[1]["alt"])','True\nTrue\nView shipment status','Distinguish a decorative image from an image-only navigation control.',['Declare each image’s contextual purpose.','Check explicit alternative attributes.','Compare empty decoration with a functional label.'],'Presence checks do not establish that the supplied descriptions are contextually accurate or complete.'),mistakes:['Using the filename as the default informative alternative.','Treating every chart as decorative because a paragraph exists nearby.'],takeaways:['Choose alternatives according to contextual purpose.','Give complex information an equivalent accessible representation.'],quiz:[
['What is appropriate alt for a purely decorative image?','An explicit empty string','A missing attribute always','The full file path','A random keyword list','An empty alternative deliberately removes unnecessary decorative announcements.','Omission is not the same as decorative intent.','Paths are not useful decorative content.','Keywords do not provide meaningful equivalence.'],
['What should an image-only link’s alternative explain?','Its purpose or destination','Only its pixel dimensions','Only its compression ratio','Only its designer’s name','The image supplies the link’s meaningful content, so the alternative must explain its function.','Dimensions do not identify the destination.','Compression is not navigation purpose.','Authorship is not the action users need.'],
['Why can a complex chart need more than a short alt?','Its information may be richer','Every chart is a password','Alt always executes JavaScript','Tables cannot show chart values','A concise alternative can identify the chart while nearby text or tables convey detailed relationships.','Charts are not inherently credentials.','Alt is text, not script execution.','Accessible tables can convey values.'],
['A report image is named chart-final-7.png. What should determine its alternative?','The information it conveys','The filename alone','The upload date alone','The image directory alone','Contextual meaning and purpose determine a useful equivalent.','Filenames are often uninformative.','Dates do not describe the data relationships.','Directories do not explain content.'],
['A decorative separator has missing alt and is announced by filename. What can fix it?','Provide alt=""','Add the filename twice','Make the file name longer','Remove all page headings','An explicit empty alternative identifies the decoration as unnecessary to announce.','Repeating noise makes it worse.','A longer name is not meaningful equivalence.','Heading removal harms unrelated structure.'],
['A chart shows the only evidence of late-delivery trends. What should be available?','Equivalent accessible information','Only a decorative empty alt','Only the chart’s dimensions','Only the chart software version','Users unable to perceive the chart still need its substantive information.','Empty alt would hide essential meaning.','Size does not convey the trends.','Software version does not explain the data.'],
['An icon is a link to tracking but alt says “blue circle.” What is missing?','The navigation purpose','The exact CSS shade','The source-control branch','The image’s byte count','For a functional image, the useful alternative identifies the link’s purpose.','Colour precision does not explain destination.','Branches do not label navigation.','File size is not purpose.'],
['An automated check confirms every img has alt. What remains necessary?','Contextual quality review','Assume all alternatives are useful','Certify every screen-reader flow','Ignore every complex image','Attribute presence cannot establish truthful, concise and context-appropriate alternatives.','Text can exist yet be unhelpful.','No complete assistive interaction was tested.','Complex information may need additional representation.']
]},
{id:'html-document-metadata',comp:'m3.c2',title:'Write useful titles, descriptions and sharing metadata',introduction:'A university event page appears in browser tabs, search results and shared links. Clear metadata helps people identify the page, but it does not guarantee search ranking or force every external service to display the same preview.',sections:[s('Separate document metadata from visible content','The head contains metadata such as title, character encoding declarations and links to resources. A concise, distinctive title should identify the current page and relevant site context. A meta description can summarize the page, but search engines may choose other text; keyword stuffing is not a substitute for useful content.','Declare the document language on html and use appropriate character encoding, normally UTF-8. A viewport meta declaration such as width=device-width, initial-scale=1 supports responsive behavior but does not create a responsive layout by itself. Do not disable user zoom merely to make a screenshot fit.'),s('Treat sharing previews as external consumers','Social preview systems may read conventions such as Open Graph title, description, image and URL properties. Use accurate public information and suitable images. A preview can be cached and its rendering depends on the consuming platform, so changing a tag is not proof that every shared card updated immediately.','The parser fixture collects a title and description from controlled markup. It does not contact a search engine, measure ranking or validate every sharing convention. Check actual page metadata and intended consumer behavior under permitted read-only tests.')],worked:w('from html.parser import HTMLParser\nclass Metadata(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self): super().__init__(); self.description=None; self.in_title=False; self.title=""\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        a=dict(attrs)\n        if tag=="title": self.in_title=True\n        if tag=="meta" and a.get("name")=="description": self.description=a.get("content")\n    def handle_endtag(self, tag):\n        if tag=="title": self.in_title=False\n    def handle_data(self, data):\n        if self.in_title: self.title += data\np=Metadata(); p.feed("""<head><title>Open Day | Example University</title><meta name=\"description\" content=\"Plan your campus visit.\"></head>""")\nprint(p.title)\nprint(p.description)','Open Day | Example University\nPlan your campus visit.','Extract a page title and concise public description.',['Recognize the title context.','Read the named description metadata.','Compare both values with the page’s actual purpose.'],'The parser checks supplied text only. External search and sharing platforms may render or cache metadata differently.'),mistakes:['Claiming a description guarantees search ranking.','Disabling zoom to hide layout problems.'],takeaways:['Make titles and descriptions accurate and distinctive.','Check consumer behavior without overstating metadata guarantees.'],quiz:[
['Where is a title element normally placed?','The document head','Inside every input value','Only in the URL query','Only in a stylesheet rule','Title is document metadata in the head, separate from visible page headings.','Input values are submitted/control data.','A query is not document title metadata.','CSS does not define the title element.'],
['What does a meta description provide?','A page summary candidate','A guaranteed search rank','Server authorization rules','A mandatory identical preview','Search systems can use descriptions but may choose other snippets and ranking signals.','A summary does not guarantee position.','Metadata does not enforce permissions.','External consumers can behave differently.'],
['What does lang on html describe?','The document language','The deployment branch','The image compression method','The SQL connection encoding','A language declaration helps user agents and assistive technology interpret content.','Branches are source-control state.','Compression is not language.','Database connection settings are separate.'],
['Every site page has the title “Home.” What should improve?','Distinctive page identification','Only the title’s font colour','Only the server disk size','Only the image dimensions','Specific titles help users distinguish tabs, history entries and search results.','The title metadata is not styled as visible page text.','Storage capacity does not identify pages.','Image sizing does not distinguish titles.'],
['A team changes Open Graph tags but old cards remain. What could explain this?','Consumer caching and behavior','The HTML must be empty','Every user is unauthorized','The CSS cascade erased metadata','External preview systems may cache information and choose their own rendering.','A stale preview does not prove empty HTML.','Authorization is not established by preview staleness.','CSS does not rewrite source metadata.'],
['A viewport declaration is added to a fixed-width layout. What else is needed?','Responsive layout work','A promise all widths now fit','Disabling every user zoom option','Deleting semantic headings','Viewport metadata supports mobile behavior but does not fix inflexible CSS layouts.','Metadata alone cannot guarantee fit.','Zoom should not be disabled as a repair.','Heading removal does not solve layout constraints.'],
['A description includes private applicant information. What should happen?','Replace it with public summary','Assume the head is confidential','Hide it with CSS only','Keep it because search helps privacy','Metadata is delivered to clients and may be indexed or shared, so it must avoid private records.','Head content is inspectable.','CSS does not make delivered metadata secret.','Indexing can broaden exposure.'],
['The metadata parser extracts a title successfully. What is not established?','Actual search-result ranking','The supplied title text','The description’s parsed value','That a title tag was read','Local parsing does not contact a search engine or determine ranking behavior.','The title is the direct output.','The description is explicitly extracted.','The parser handles the title context.']
]},
{id:'html-media-links-review',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Review linked media and contextual alternatives',introduction:'A customer help page combines a troubleshooting screenshot, a download link and a short instructional video. The content must remain understandable when images fail, users navigate by keyboard or media cannot be heard.',sections:[s('Give links and media honest expectations','Link text should explain the destination or action; a downloadable document can identify its format and relevant size when useful. Do not surprise users with an unrelated new context or a misleading label. A link wrapping an image needs an appropriate accessible name from its content or another suitable association.','For informative screenshots, explain the essential step in nearby text as well as choosing concise alternative text. Do not expose real account identifiers, patient details or session values in instructional captures. Synthetic data can demonstrate the workflow without turning training material into a privacy disclosure.'),s('Review alternatives in the complete page','Audio/video may require captions, transcripts or other appropriate alternatives depending on its information. Captions are not the same as a decorative alt attribute; the medium and content determine the needed equivalent. Autoplaying sound and inaccessible custom controls can make an otherwise useful tutorial difficult to use.','The fixture compares available text alternatives for declared media purposes. It is a checklist model, not a complete WCAG conformance decision. Actual review needs the content, timing, control behavior, keyboard interaction and user context, not merely a nonempty property.')],worked:w('media=[{"kind":"screenshot", "alternative":"Open Settings, then choose Notifications."},{"kind":"video", "captions":True, "transcript":"Choose a delivery preference and save."}]\nprint(bool(media[0]["alternative"]))\nprint(media[1]["captions"] and bool(media[1]["transcript"]))\nprint("review context and controls separately")','True\nTrue\nreview context and controls separately','Check that declared instructional media has relevant alternative material.',['Read each medium’s intended purpose.','Check the supplied alternative fields.','Keep actual equivalence and control testing separate.'],'Nonempty fields are not proof of accurate captions, complete transcripts or accessible media controls.'),mistakes:['Using real private records in tutorial screenshots.','Calling a nonempty captions field a complete accessibility audit.'],takeaways:['Explain media purpose and provide meaningful equivalents.','Review content privacy and actual control behavior together.'],quiz:[
['What should useful download-link text communicate?','The expected destination or file','Only “click here” everywhere','Only a random identifier','Only the border colour','Links should make their destination or file purpose understandable in context.','Repeated generic labels lose meaning outside nearby prose.','Random values do not explain purpose.','Colour is not a destination description.'],
['What is safer for an instructional screenshot?','Synthetic nonprivate records','A real user’s session cookie','An unredacted patient chart','A full private inbox','Synthetic data can demonstrate the workflow without disclosing real private information.','Session values are credentials.','Medical records are sensitive.','Private messages are not necessary for generic training.'],
['Are captions equivalent to an img alt attribute?','No, medium and purpose differ','Yes, any text field is identical','Yes, one word covers all media','Only when the file is large','Different media carry information in different ways and require suitable equivalents.','Text fields are not automatically interchangeable.','One word may omit essential information.','File size does not define semantic equivalence.'],
['A tutorial screenshot fails to load. What can preserve the essential instruction?','Clear nearby step text','Only the filename','Only a decorative empty alt','Only a fixed pixel width','Important task instructions should not exist solely inside an unavailable image.','Filenames are often not explanatory.','Empty alternatives hide rather than convey information.','Width does not communicate the step.'],
['A video has a captions flag but inaccurate words. What should review conclude?','The content still needs correction','The flag proves conformance','Only the poster image matters','The transcript must be deleted','Presence metadata cannot replace checking the accuracy and completeness of alternatives.','A boolean is not a semantic audit.','Poster images do not carry all spoken information.','Removing useful alternatives is not the repair.'],
['A linked icon says only “arrow” in alt. What should be reconsidered?','Its functional accessible name','Only the arrow’s angle','Only its compression quality','Only its file extension','A functional image needs to explain what following the link does.','Geometry alone does not identify destination.','Compression does not label a link.','Extensions do not convey purpose.'],
['A media player can only be operated with a mouse. What remains deficient?','Keyboard-operable controls','The public domain length','The number of comments','The image metadata order','People using keyboards need to access and operate relevant media controls.','Host length is unrelated to interaction.','Comment count does not establish access.','Metadata ordering does not implement controls.'],
['The media checklist prints True twice. What claim is too broad?','Complete accessibility compliance','The supplied fields were present','The fixture read captions=True','The instruction string was nonempty','A small presence check does not evaluate the full page, media accuracy or interaction requirements.','Presence is what the code checks.','That value is supplied fixture data.','The string check is explicitly executed.']
]}
];
